--TEST--
A method call is refused wherever the receiver's type names no class — a union, a scalar, an `array<T>`, a `void` call — and a receiver that may be `null` is still one mistake — `rule:types/grammar`, `rule:types/erased-member-access`
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Dog {
    public function speak(): string { return "woof"; }
}

final class Cat {
    public function speak(): string { return "meow"; }
}

final class V {
    public static function nothing(): void {}
}

// Accepted, and written first so that a spelling that stops resolving fails
// here rather than as a missing refusal: a receiver that names one class
// resolves against it, and `rule:types/unions-and-mixed`'s `is` narrowing recovers
// that class out of a union.
Dog $d = new Dog();
echo $d->speak(), "\n";

Dog|Cat $narrowed = new Cat();
if ($narrowed is Cat) {
    echo $narrowed->speak(), "\n";
}

// A method is resolved against a class, and a union names several or none —
// there is no one signature to check the argument list against, and no one
// return type for the call's position to take. `is` and `as` are both
// in the help because both already lower.
Dog|Cat $u = new Dog();
echo $u->speak(), "\n";

// A type that can hold no object at all has nothing to narrow to, so the help
// is the property half's instead: convert, or declare the receiver `mixed`,
// which `rule:types/conversion` makes the one unchecked position.
string $s = "x";
echo $s->speak(), "\n";

array<int> $xs = [1];
echo $xs->speak(), "\n";

// A `void` call yields no value, so neither fix applies.
echo V::nothing()->speak(), "\n";

// A receiver that may be `null` whose non-`null` half *is* a class is one
// mistake and takes one code: `E0459` on the way in, and no second refusal
// behind it.
?Dog $maybe = null;
echo $maybe->speak(), "\n";
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0477]: `Dog|Cat` names no class, so it has no method `speak`
   --> %s:31:6
   |
31 | echo $u->speak(), "\n";
   |      ^^^^^^^^^ called on a receiver that names no class here
   = help: narrow the receiver to the class that declares `speak` first — `if ($x is ClassName) { … }`, or `$x as ClassName`; `rule:types/grammar` makes `object` the opaque top of every class type, and `rule:types/erased-member-access` erases a property access through one but not a call

error[E0477]: `string` names no class, so it has no method `speak`
   --> %s:37:6
   |
37 | echo $s->speak(), "\n";
   |      ^^^^^^^^^ called on a receiver that names no class here
   = help: only an object has methods — convert the receiver to the class that declares `speak` (`$x as Box`), or declare it `mixed`, which is the one unchecked position (`rule:types/conversion`) and defers the whole question to a catchable throw at run time

error[E0477]: `array<int>` names no class, so it has no method `speak`
   --> %s:40:6
   |
40 | echo $xs->speak(), "\n";
   |      ^^^^^^^^^^ called on a receiver that names no class here
   = help: only an object has methods — convert the receiver to the class that declares `speak` (`$x as Box`), or declare it `mixed`, which is the one unchecked position (`rule:types/conversion`) and defers the whole question to a catchable throw at run time

error[E0477]: `void` names no class, so it has no method `speak`
   --> %s:43:6
   |
43 | echo V::nothing()->speak(), "\n";
   |      ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ called on a receiver that names no class here
   = help: a call that returns `void` yields no value, so there is nothing for `speak` to be called on — call `speak` on the receiver you meant

error[E0459]: `null|Dog` may be `null`, so `->` cannot reach a member of it
   --> %s:49:6
   |
49 | echo $maybe->speak(), "\n";
   |      ^^^^^^ this receiver is nullable
   = help: test it first — inside `if ($x != null) { … }` the receiver is no longer nullable — or use `?->`, which answers `null` instead of reaching the member

